package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setEdgeOfEternities = StorySection(
    "eoe", "Edge of Eternities",
    "Out past the walls of the Multiverse a dying star was killed on purpose, a stone that could rewrite the past woke in the clay of a dwarf world, and a broke young captain went looking for a lost cat.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-the-edge", "The Edge",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-beyond-the-walls", "Beyond the Walls",
                    listOf(
                        "Edge of Eternities came out on August 1, 2025, under the set code EOE. It was the 106th Magic expansion and the first that left the surface of worlds behind and went out among the stars. Its codename during design was Volleyball, Wizards first teased it at Gen Con in August 2023, and when they announced it properly they were careful to say that it was a space set but very much science fantasy rather than science fiction. Ethan Fleischer led the design with Mark Rosewater beside him, and Andrew Brown led development. It followed Final Fantasy onto shelves and came just before Marvel's Spider-Man.",
                        "In the story it sits after the sets of the [Omenpath](the-omenpaths) era, the run of worlds that ended with [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm), and it does something no set had done before. It leaves the Multiverse entirely. The Edge is a single enormous plane wrapped around the outside of the Multiverse, a universe of billions of solar systems that borders the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities) without knowing it. Its peoples have never heard of planeswalkers, of the Phyrexians, of any of it. To them it is simply space, and the far side of the barrier at the inner edge of their sky is a place of myths and devils.",
                        "Only one visitor from the Multiverse is known to have arrived. Tezzeret, the artificer with the metal body, was thrown out of the Blind Eternities by some means nobody has explained, and he washed up in the Edge as a piece of drifting debris. He found it soft, rich and full of opportunity, and by the time the story begins he has made himself a criminal power there, fencing stolen goods and taking a cut of everything he can reach.",
                        "The story was told in eleven episodes by Seth Dickinson between June 20 and July 7, 2025, and it was told in an unusual way. It is narrated by the artifact at its heart, which speaks to the reader directly, asks the reader to make choices on its behalf, and numbers each version of events as a revision. When the reader chooses wrongly, it shows the failed timeline and sends them back. The tabs on this page follow the story the way it finally went.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-a-ring-of-creation", "A Ring of Creation",
                    listOf(
                        "Every people who belong to Pinnacle, the great alliance that runs travel between the stars, is taught the same picture of the universe, and they call that shared picture the Common Agreement. Reality is a concert of particles and substrates, aether and mana, photons and quarks. Most peoples who reached space evolved on solid worlds, and those worlds orbit stars made of concentrated mana, aether, hydrogen, helium and trace gases. Some of these arrangements turn out perfect enough to sustain life.",
                        "The shape of the whole universe was worked out only recently, and it is a ring. Look one way along the observable arc and the light shifts faintly blue, and eventually observation ends at a chaotic, scrambled barrier of howling, recombinant particles that nothing can see past. The people of the Edge call it the Chaos Wall. Look the other way and every galaxy is slipping into the red, moving away, and the universe grows so fast that observation simply gives out. For a sense of symmetry that border is called the Quiet Wall. The Edge lies between them, between the raw destructive power of creation and the slow quiet death of entropy, which is how it got its popular name, the Edge of Eternity.",
                        "Pinnacle's scholars suspect that something lies behind the Chaos Wall, because faster-than-light travel has to pierce it to work, and because now and again colossal bursts of chaotic ejecta come spitting back out of it carrying aetheric waste and strange particles that decay almost at once. Drix myth fills in what science cannot. In those stories the lamellae beyond the wall are hellish places, the homes of ancient beasts and devils that raged across the Edge millennia ago.",
                        "The reader knows what the Chaos Wall really is. It is the outer face of the Multiverse, and Tezzeret, reading Pinnacle's primer on it, recognized himself in the description of ejecta. His right hand Mm'menon had told him there were rumours of technology that could cross the wall, and he wanted it found.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We are the fodder of children's tales, then, are we? Good, let's play on that as we drip-feed information on my origins to our agents. But I'm not interested in ejecta, Mm'menon. I am ejecta.",
                        "Tezzeret, a note in the margin of the Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-ships-and-the-weft", "Ships and the Weft",
                    listOf(
                        "Space in the Edge is measured in time more than distance, and the gulfs between stars are so wide that crossing them below the speed of light is possible but useless. Travel inside a system is ordinary. Ships run on engines that are usually nuclear and usually fuelled by moxite, a rare substance rich in magical energy that drives interstellar flight, and they range from one-person craft to vessels with thousands aboard. Tezzeret, looking at moxite for the first time, thought it not unlike the etherium of his own body and decided the locals had barely scratched what it could do.",
                        "To cross between stars a ship has to slip out of ordinary space into a parallel layer of reality that Pinnacle calls the Eternities and the Drix call the Weft. Three things are needed. The ship must carry an eternity drive, it must be close to an eternity column, and it must have a Pinnacle codebook to line up where it starts and where it ends. An eternity drive does not actually move a ship through the Weft. It only opens and closes the door. The column does the throwing.",
                        "An eternity column is a free-floating installation kilometres long that exists in the same place in every layer of reality at once, and ships are slung from one column to the next like cable cars running along a track. The columns are built and controlled by Pinnacle alone. Most ships carry no eternity drive at all, so they dock with Pinnacle ferries at a column and ride across that way. A column works one way, too. It is a ramp for leaving a system and only a beacon for arriving, since a ship needs no column to drop back out of the Weft.",
                        "Tezzeret, who used to cross the Multiverse unaided, found the whole arrangement both familiar and insulting. He compared the columns to the Planar Bridge, wondered whether they might carry him back through the Chaos Wall, and sent his agents to steal a codebook.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-pinnacle", "Pinnacle",
                    listOf(
                        "Pinnacle began as an alliance between several spacefaring species from the same solar system, who learned of each other through radio before any of them had left home. In their second century of expanding outward they met the Drix, who could already walk between the stars, and the Drix shared enough of that secret for Pinnacle to build the first eternity columns and drives. In exchange the Drix asked for authority over anything that came through from outside the universe, and for the right to search every dying star that Pinnacle reached. Pinnacle's founding purpose has stayed the same ever since, to organize the universe under a single body whose mandate is peace, cooperation and shared prosperity.",
                        "Pinnacle has no money. Because it was created by treaty with the Drix, who can simply walk away from any bargain they dislike and so recognize no economy built on bondage or compulsion, its members trade in mallowmass instead. Mallowmass is the reputation a person earns by doing good work in public, and it binds the duties of host and guest very tightly. Its emblem is a purple flower. To claim mallowmass on someone's ship or station is to put yourself under their protection, and to break it is an outrage almost everywhere.",
                        "Day to day, Pinnacle is something most people meet at a column. Its Strategic Corps are the directors, emissaries and ambassadors who hold real power. Its Tactical Corps are the thousands of ordinary specialists who fly the ships, process the documents, run the clinics and cook the food, everything Pinnacle calls tactics. Every director is paired with a Checkmate, part first officer and part adversarial counsel, chosen to think as differently from the director as possible. Tezzeret read all of this and saw the threat underneath it, that a world which will not cooperate can simply be cut off from the stars.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "All these worlds are yours / But they are older than you / Older than you know",
                        "The warning the Drix gave Pinnacle with the gift of star travel, Edge of Eternities, Episode 10",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-who-lives-here", "Who Lives Out Here",
                    listOf(
                        "Machines are everywhere in the Edge, and the word for them is mechan. A drone is a mechan with no mind of its own, a tool however complicated its work. An android is a mechan that is a whole person, conscious and self-aware, and most civilizations that make androids give them the same rights as anyone else, though some forbid making them at all. An organic person can become an android by surgery or by having their consciousness transferred, and some societies grow digital minds from nothing.",
                        "Most of the artificial intelligences that run stations, colonies and ships are viys. A viy is brilliant in the way a slime mould is brilliant. It can solve enormously tangled problems with many moving parts, but it has no picture of the world behind the data and no common sense at all. That matters on Sigma, where a colony viy calmly explains away every sign that something terrible has happened.",
                        "The peoples are as varied as the machines. Eumidians, insectlike and endlessly adaptable, are so common that many think of them as the ordinary shape of life between the walls. Humans are a curious case. Something once pushed them almost to extinction and they regrew from the survivors, so they are unusually alike, and the Edge thinks of them as obligate mimics who survive by copying a culture rather than by adapting their bodies. That makes them easy recruits for great faiths. The Kav of Kavaron are huge and tough and read the ground through their bellies, the Illvoi are jellyfish from the depths of gas giants, the Drix walk between stars, and the Astelli are made of light. All of them meet in Sothera.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities, by Miguel Lopez and others, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1, 2 and 10, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Edge"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-sothera", "The Sothera System",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-dying-star", "The Dying Star",
                    listOf(
                        "Sothera was a sun, billions of years old, and the mother of five worlds, Susur Secundi, Kavaron, Evendo, Adagia and the giant Uthros. Some time in her long life she strayed into a vast cloud of interstellar aether and began to eat it, hungrily, more than she could hold. She grew too big and too bright and her light turned strange. Her spasms scoured her nearest children, dissolving one world to atoms and withering another to its core, and the nebula crowded in around her until the night sky of every one of her worlds was smeared with cosmic gas. In less than two hundred years she would go nova and strip the air from every planet she had.",
                        "The Kav, the only people native to the system, were the first to understand what was coming, and they began a desperate effort to lift their whole species off their homeworld. Their furious activity drew Pinnacle, which built an eternity column called Infinite Guideline and opened the doomed system to the rest of the Edge. The Drix always come to dying stars for one last mysterious inspection, so everyone waited for them to arrive and give the final word. They gave no sign. Sothera became an end of days boomtown, full of people hoping to earn mallow by helping with the evacuation, studying the coming blast, or mining what would soon be lost.",
                        "Then the Monoists arrived and asked what nobody else had dared. What if Sothera did not have to explode at all? They gave the star sekhar, a bead that induces a singularity, and turned her collapse inward. Sothera became a supervoid, a black hole sun, and the Monoists claimed her as the thousandth supervoid of their faith. A supervoid is not entirely dark. The gas Sothera had been gathering for her explosion is caught in an eerie violet ring around her called a photon collar, still bright enough to warm the inner worlds, feed Kavaron's glasshouse crops and glitter off Adagia's mirrors, though too weak to keep carbon life alive on Uthros.",
                        "The system was saved and the Kav's world was still lost. Worse, the Monoists are feeding their dark child to make it grow, and the Celestial Palatinate has sent a fleet to turn her back into a star. The card {Sothera, the Supervoid} was chosen as the set's headliner, its single biggest face.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-susur-secundi", "Susur Secundi",
                    listOf(
                        "The innermost world is a dark, eerie place of bare black stone, crossed by labyrinths and monumental rock formations that look shaped by some long-dead people because their lines are too straight to be natural. Its air is thin and it lies open to everything space throws at it. The Kav called it Anuki, the growler, explored it centuries ago, found nothing worth mining and left it alone.",
                        "When the Monoists came they found that its labyrinths, columns and gravitational tides matched their hymns and scriptures, and they took the planet as a sign that their prophet had prepared it for them. The Kav did not contest the claim. The faithful renamed it Susur Secundi after the capital of their order, the great monastery at the Lip of Susur, and made it the staging ground for their whole project in Sothera. A monastery is being built in orbit above it. Below, monitoring stations and flight towers dot the surface, and the faithful live in tunnels dug fresh or taken over from old Kav missions. The ancient labyrinths themselves are holy ground, kept for study, worship and contemplation, never for living in.",
                        "Few outsiders are allowed to land there, though neutral stations hang in orbit selling water ice and watching the war. Inside, the life of the faithful is strange and intense. Acolytes sleep alone in tanks of warm salt water. Miners come off shift to sing and dance, and the young party hard on a drug called shride. Every Monoist chooses for themselves, and their whole society is built on absolute respect for individual will, which is exactly why they can ask their own people to volunteer for death. {Susur Secundi, Void Altar} is its Planet card.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-kavaron", "Kavaron",
                    listOf(
                        "Kavaron was once green and rich. The Kav mined it for moxite for centuries, and when they learned their sun was going to explode they mined it harder still, hard enough to lift their people and much of their living world into space. They tore the planet open. Whole continents rose and slumped into the wound, and Kavaron split in two. The debris made a ring around the planet that still falls on its people, and the Kav call it Duranuw, the Mordraine Ring, because it is the stone rain left by a murder.",
                        "The Kav divide what is left in two. Kavaron Before is the last stable wedge of the world, held together by a ring of titanic tectonic anchors driven deep into the crust. Inside that wall the ancient capital Vu still stands, crowded with refugees and with every monument and treasure the Kav could carry to it, a nation-sized museum where shuttles take off day and night. Outside the wall is Kavaron That Is, a churning storm of rubble under a hurricane sky, where the ground still quakes and explodes and falling mountains crash down because the Guns of Kavaron, huge lasers on Kavaron Before, nudge them away from the inhabited half and onto the ruined one.",
                        "Kavaron That Is is still the richest ground in Sothera, so it draws everyone. Wildcat crews hunt the scraps of moxite too small for the big concerns, scavengers raid the hardened memorial vaults where the Kav buried treasures they could not move, and Kav memorial teams race the scavengers to save what belongs to their people. Mining towns like Taro-duend live on stilts there, out at the edge of the law. The Kav know it is doomed and so is the other half. As the Kav put it, eventually all of Kavaron Before will become Kavaron That Is. Its Planet card is {Kavaron, Memorial World}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Even until Exodus Day, those in power on Kavaron refused to acknowledge that the planet was trying to tell them something.",
                        "Remnant Elemental",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-evendo", "Evendo",
                    listOf(
                        "Evendo orbits Sothera almost exactly opposite Kavaron, and the early Kav explorers wrote it off as a cold dead world of ice. They were wrong. Eons ago a Eumidian seedship had passed by and planted a brood there, seed pods buried beneath the glaciers, waiting for conditions good enough to begin their great work. When Kavaron broke apart its debris rained down on Evendo, churned and melted the ancient ice, and warmed the world until it grew a thin, stable atmosphere. The warmth woke the first Eumidians from their long sleep.",
                        "For decades since, the Eumidians have been remaking Evendo and themselves together, a process they call terrasymbiosis. Their enclaves are lush pockets of life surrounded by a slowly melting perimeter of ice, and outside them the glaciers and the titanic beasts that sleep in them still rule. Along the equator the change is complete, a band of atmosphere-piercing forests laced with Eumidian hive cities that shows what the rest of the world will become. Not every creature welcomed the thaw.",
                        "Evendo's success has been noticed. The Kav, who have no home, have begun to see in it a Kavaron Tomorrow and are already drawing up plans to take it once the Eumidians have finished. The settled Eumidians mostly do not know it, though the ones who fly in space have their suspicions, and a living seedship called the Hearthhull watches over the brood from above. {Evendo, Waking Haven} is the world's Planet card, and it became one of the most played cards of the whole set in Commander.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We will not yield until the Eumidians relinquish our Kavaron Tomorrow.",
                        "Official Kav diplomatic statement, Diplomatic Relations",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-adagia-and-uthros", "Adagia and Uthros",
                    listOf(
                        "Adagia, which was once called Adawa, is a world of powerful, endless wind. The wind has sculpted its land, and its plants and animals have grown sleek and aerodynamic to ride it. Sail-like windskippers, Adagian arrows, sky minnows and dart raptors flit around the low wind-powered buildings of its colonists, and bigger creatures drift high in its upper air. Its atmosphere is thin and cold enough that a human can breathe it only at real risk, but it is rich in life and so attracts the drug and farming companies of the Celestial Palatinate. When the Sunstar Free Company arrived the settlers were already loyal to the Palatinate, and the knights simply declared themselves its administrators. From the inner system it shows as a bright point of light, the world of mirrors. {Adagia, Windswept Bastion} is its Planet card.",
                        "Uthros is a titanic gas giant ringed by dozens of moons, the first world a ship meets coming in from outside and the last one it passes going out. Its sapphire upper atmosphere is crowded with moxite refineries, supply stations and ship-breaking yards, and full of stratowhales, living anvil rays and troporeefs drifting on sacs of gas. Below the clouds it turns into a crushing blue hell where diamonds fall like rain on continents of compressed gas and rivers of liquid metallic hydrogen cut valleys through ice.",
                        "In those depths the Illvoi of the Uthros Combine have built a secret research complex. Officially they study atmospheric engineering. Nobody quite believes it, and the cards hint at more. A Combine log on {Uthros Psionicist} records psionic activity from the planet's core, fragmentary and full of isolation, and says that it wants free. The Planet card {Uthros, Titanic Godcore} gives the core a name that suggests what the Combine suspects.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-outer-dark", "The Outer Dark",
                    listOf(
                        "Pinnacle's gate to Sothera is Infinite Guideline, an eternity column and the station grown around it. Seen from a patrol drone it is a great starfish of blue aurora and white alloy, the blade of the column driven through its hub, parked at a balancing point behind Uthros where the pull of Sothera and the giant cancel out. Ships arrive and leave there, peoples from across the Edge mingle there, and everything there is watched over by Pinnacle.",
                        "Farther out is a stretch of space nobody understands. The Garden of Apeiron is a vast region full of anomalies and strange phenomena. Ghostly signals come out of it speaking random words, and some captains swear they hear personal messages buried inside. Ships go in and never come out, and probes and the deepest scans return nothing but static. It sits between the orbit of Uthros and the outer wall, and worst of all it is moving, crawling inward toward Sothera faster than any drift should allow. Somewhere inside it, the legendary eternity ship Inspirit lies lost.",
                        "The edge of the system is the Wurmwall, an asteroid belt that marks everything inside as Sotheran and everything beyond as deep space. It is a maze of jagged debris, living geodes, wandering comets and derelict ships, and it is named for the greatwurms, titanic creatures of great age and wisdom that live in it. Pinnacle advises every ship to keep well clear. Years before the story, a research ship called the Wurm Speaker went out to talk to the voices in the Wurmwall. Few came back.",
                        "There are smaller places too. The dwarf planet Sigma lies between Uthros and Kavaron, and on it is the colony of Sigma's Reach, built to mine moxite clay. According to every record it never opened, because nobody wanted to move to a star that was about to explode. That is where the story begins.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1, 3 and 5, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, The Edge"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall, Edge of Eternities card text"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-faiths-at-war", "Faiths at War",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-monoism", "Monoism",
                    listOf(
                        "Monoism is an ancient faith that teaches one truth. The purpose of the universe is to make supervoids. When the stars have gone out and creation has frozen, only supervoids will remain, and inside each of them a new and better universe is born, a world without walls that the faithful call the Next Eternity or the World to Come. INEVITA is their name for the perfect future beyond, and they teach that it has already happened and is only waiting for time to catch up. Their work is to hasten it by binding newborn supervoids together into one great ensemble and feeding them, so that everything they swallow goes into the same forever.",
                        "The faith is ruled by the Monasteriat, a congress of hundreds of elector monks seated at the Lip of Susur, the glowing collar around Point Prime, the first and holiest supervoid. The Susurians who founded the faith were born from the torn body of the star that became Point Prime, massive beings who bend light around themselves and command gravity by nature. Most Monoists today are human, though the faith takes anyone. At the centre of worship is the Immortal Faller, the prophet seen forever falling toward the heart of every supervoid, whose song the faithful hear and try to interpret. His book The Seeker and the Well and the ever-growing The Theorem Unending and Final make up their scripture, The Plummet Record.",
                        "Monoism respects individual will absolutely, and it does not pretend to be anything other than what it is. Faithful who are called may take the Plummet into a supervoid and go ahead to paradise, and soldiers carry a sekhar bead, a tiny void that will collapse them into a black hole when they choose, carrying them straight to the Next Eternity. Their warriors wear alabile armor, a metal hammered from condensed events that can swallow an attack and store it, and their gravkill paladins kill with tides of gravity. Their motto is short. Nothing tests our faith.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Sing! When the Faller reaches the end of his journey, all things will arrive at the Zero Point. What is unending will persist through to the new creation and be ordered accordingly.",
                        "The Theorem Unending and Final, Antiphon 6, on Gravkill",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-bright-sum", "The Bright Sum",
                    listOf(
                        "The Celestial Palatinate is an ancient empire of many species and worlds, older than Pinnacle and older than faster-than-light travel, and most of its subjects are human. It is an opulent dynastic monarchy ruled by a divine emperor, the Regent Maximum Taman IV, whose holy office stands above the merely imperial state. Beneath the Regent rule the Cosmogrands, near-absolute lords of at least one world each, whose power depends on how rich and developed their holdings are and how many ships and knights they field. A Cosmogrand would rather rule one thriving world than a vast claim of empty space.",
                        "Its state faith is Summism, the faith of the Sum. Summists believe the beginning of the universe is its brightest and most precious time. The universe will one day run out of aether to make new stars, so every star kept alive, and every bit of light and metal made early, is a gift to everything that comes after. They tabulate the data of the universe and follow the figure that results, the Bright Sum, as a compass toward the most good over the longest time. Its physics is the Grand Cosmocordance, which teaches that only the present is real, that the past cannot be altered and the future does not yet exist. To act against it is anstruth, a blasphemy against reality itself.",
                        "This is a faith of science and also a faith of terrible arithmetic. If a vast enough future good is at stake, Summism can demand almost any present cost. The story's heroine, a young squire, is raised to love that clarity, and the whole of her arc is the moment she sees what it can be used to justify.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The light does not ask permission to cut through the darkness.",
                        "Mentor Ultimate, to Regent Maximum Taman IV, Radiant Strike",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-sunstar-free-company", "Sunstar Free Company",
                    listOf(
                        "The Palatinate fights through free companies, mercenary armies that serve causes rather than pay. Some are a single ship. The greatest of them is the Sunstar Free Company, the Regent Maximum's favoured regiment and in practice the state army, with hundreds of thousands of knights and millions of foot soldiers across many fronts. Its troops come up from the common folk of the Cosmogrands' worlds. The foot soldiers are called photophoroi. A rare few, mostly noble children, become squires, serving a knight as attendant and guard until they might earn knighthood themselves.",
                        "Its knights are formidable. A Solar Knight fights with twin bladiators, hardlight blades that also fire blasts of solar energy, and draws power from incaglas, the light-carrying material woven through every Sunstar machine. Knights burn their bodies hot, sweat in saunas and gulp salt water, and on the shieldship Candela everyone is stripped of hair in a painful ritual of molten wax. A squire wraps her knight in himsary banners covered in oaths before he puts on his armor, and they face the nearest star and speak the Litany of Dawn together.",
                        "The company also keeps the anathalmanac, a catalogue of terrors written in blood. One entry describes an object that can make a person into someone else and swallow all their deeds, and it lists the rituals a knight must follow to stay fixed in place against it, each chosen by some fact of the stars or of a birth date so that no knight can choose for themselves. Its seventh protocol is to destroy yourself, everyone near you and the object, by the most violent means available. On Kavaron the company follows it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Praise the dawn light, praise for the morning / As it rises, so do we rise / Praise the dawn light, spark of creation / As it rises, so we all rise",
                        "The Litany of Dawn, Edge of Eternities, Episode 3",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-astelli", "The Astelli",
                    listOf(
                        "The Astelli are beings of hard light, the living remains of stars swallowed by supervoids. When a star collapses, tears of mana slip away from its photon collar and come together as living things, and each Astelli carries the memories of the star that bore it. Siblings from the same dead sun live in close communities and hope to carry its fire forward. They can only be born from the ruin of a star that a supervoid has eaten.",
                        "The first Astelli appeared when the Monoists began spreading out from Point Prime, and they fled ahead of the wave of collapsing stars. The Monoists followed, swallowing their refuges and conquering or converting the lesser peoples in their way. When the Astelli finally stood and fought, hundreds of them died without even reaching the Susurians. So they changed tactics. They settled among the peoples of living stars and taught them, warning of what was coming and preparing them for war, and those teachings became the foundations of Summism. The Palatinate keeps that a closely guarded secret, but the Sunstar knights revere the Astelli as holy figures and fight beside them.",
                        "An Astelli can phorocast, sending its light out into what is likely and reading it as it returns. Most of what they see is a wash of chaos, but when a probability spikes high enough it resolves into a true glimpse of the future. Summists say only the unalterable can be foreseen. The story adds a second possibility, that which has already been altered. Captain Slats, the Astelli who commands the Dawnsire, foresees a death in the first battle of the story, and he is right and wrong at once.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Born of dead stars, the Astelli may fight for light, but they know the void as their womb.",
                        "Lightstall Inquisitor",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-entropy-war", "The Entropy War",
                    listOf(
                        "The two faiths cannot be reconciled. The Monoists want to make more supervoids to bring INEVITA closer, and the Summists see INEVITA as an abomination and want to relight every collapsed star they can. To do it the Palatinate built horizon javelins, colossal weapons meant to undo a supervoid and make it a star again. Across Pinnacle space the two fight what Pinnacle calls the Entropy War, and Sothera has become one of its pivotal fronts, the place where the Monasteriat means to complete its thousandth supervoid and the Palatinate means to stop it.",
                        "The Palatinate's weapon in Sothera is the Dawnsire, a needle-shaped dreadnought kilometres long, built to survive passing through a supervoid's burning event horizon and carrying a horizon javelin at its heart. It cannot carry enough power for the job, so a pair of free-electron lasers at its prow, nicknamed Nacho and Nacha by their crews, fire around the supervoid on curving paths that slingshot the beams back stronger than they left, harvesting energy from the black hole itself. It is guarded by the Candela, a shieldship that orbits above it like a flying city. The card {Dawnsire, Sunstar Dreadnought} deals one hundred damage when it is fully charged, which seems about right.",
                        "Neither faith is shown as simply good. The Monoists killed a star and send their own young people to die in it. The Sunstar knights would relight a star that was about to explode and kill every living thing nearby. Pinnacle has allowed them to fight in Sothera on condition that they keep it to the inner system, because it will not risk standing between two titans. Pinnacle calls that neutrality. The story calls it a powder keg.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 3, 4 and 7, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall, Edge of Eternities flavor text"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-peoples", "Peoples of Sothera",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-kav", "The Kav",
                    listOf(
                        "The Kav are an old empire that is new to the stars. They are big, armoured and powerful, with horn-hairs on their heads, and they evolved being tossed about by larger beasts and by each other, which is why they go loose and relaxed at the moment danger arrives and why their children are not thought properly tough until they have been knocked around a bit. They read the ground with their bellies and trust touch above sight. Their footsteps fall into step across a whole crowd, a rhythm that once let them notice the approach of a wurm. Their minds keep a long, stable memory, and Tezzeret, who had thought kavu were dumb beasts found only on Dominaria, was startled to find them here as a people.",
                        "Their only government is the Kavaronic Federal Empire, which rules Kavaron Before, searches for a new world and commands the Kav Memorial Navy. The navy's ships are converted mining vessels, hardy but slow to turn, and its memorial teams police Vu, run the evacuation and go out into Kavaron That Is to bring the buried vaults home. Most Kav now live on huge stations in orbit or in nomadic mining fleets. Out in the ruined half, frontier towns follow the older Teamer ways, where a coach drives the team and a postilion steers it, and where people still use money.",
                        "Kav ethics were shaped by living with disaster, and they are very clear. A disaster is fair. It is random and uncaring, and everyone stands together against it, risking a hundred to save ten so that everyone knows they have a chance. To steer a disaster onto someone else is the worst thing a Kav can do, even to save more lives, because it means choosing who dies. Tannuk, the story's gentle Kav pilot, once did exactly that, and his people exiled him for it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Give everything and advance!",
                        "Kav naval motto, Vaultguard Trooper",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-eumidians", "The Eumidians",
                    listOf(
                        "Eumidians spread across the stars by seedship, living vessels that burn between systems scattering whole broods of seed pods onto any world they pass. Their homeworld is so distant and forgotten it may not exist any more. A pod that lands burrows into the ground and waits, sometimes for ages, until conditions are just good enough. Then it hatches vanguards, hardy pioneers who seek each other out and begin the long work of making the world liveable, feeding the building blocks of life back into the pods so that more Eumidians hatch, each generation tuned a little better to the world than the last.",
                        "They are communal but not a hive mind. There is no queen, and a brood governs itself by consensus, though Eumidians of different broods can be as alien to each other as any two species. Each Eumidian's mind is a collection of autos, small specialized selves grown through living with a particular task, so a Eumidian is quick and brilliant in her specialty and has to think slowly and deliberately outside it. They make art and industry, practise faiths and fight among themselves, and they know that no individual will live to see the work finished.",
                        "On Evendo the work is hard. Eumidians carry ice-carvers and climbing gear and weapons for the monsters that crawl out of the thawing permafrost, and some of them reverently weave the bodies of their dead back into new material so that no life is wasted. In the story the most important Eumidian is Director Mantis of Infinite Guideline, a private believer in the Sum who chose to serve Pinnacle rather than her own kind. Cards like {Terrasymbiosis} and {Icetill Explorer} show the brood at work.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "To plant the seeds of the other is to cultivate the self.",
                        "Eumidian aphorism, Eumidian Terrabotanist",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-illvoi", "The Illvoi",
                    listOf(
                        "The Illvoi are huge gelatinous jellyfish, crowned by a translucent mantle that holds the brain, who evolved deep in the crushing core of a distant gas giant. There they developed cloudsculpting, a blend of mind and machine that lets them shape gas into solid structures, and with it they built vessels to carry them up through their atmosphere and out to the stars, where they met Pinnacle and joined it for its vast libraries. To talk to humans they wear small face-sized screens that show a human face reading their mood.",
                        "In Sothera the Illvoi mostly belong to the Uthros Combine, a private research group with a secret installation deep in the giant's atmosphere. It has three branches, an Administration that keeps it running and links it to Illvoi outside the system, a large Research and Development branch that almost never leaves the core, and a Security branch that hunts spies and chases leads across the system. Whatever Research studies down there is classified even in Pinnacle's own guide.",
                        "Mm'menon was a Combine researcher who kept insisting there was more to Sothera's oddities than anything found in the clouds of Uthros, and was thrown out for it. In the first revision of the story the narrating artifact asks the reader to fascinate Mm'menon with omens, and warns that doing so will make their exile permanent. Mm'menon became Tezzeret's right hand, studied the system's anomalies, and was the one who deduced that something in Sothera was manipulating cause and effect. The rival who took their place at the Combine, Vv'viza, appears in the digital Alchemy cards.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Log entry cycle 33,507: Tenuous perception of psionic activity emanating from Uthros's core. Nature: Nonlinguistic, fragmentary. Content: Isolation, apoplexy. It wants free.",
                        "Uthros Combine research notes, Uthros Psionicist",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-drix", "The Drix",
                    listOf(
                        "The Drix are tall grey humanoids with long arms, tapered fingers and a line of blue light that divides forehead from face. Their society is millennia older than Pinnacle. They can weftwalk, stepping through the Weft while staying anchored in reality, so that all of space and time folds down to the distance of a single step, and they need no ship or column to do it. They amplify the gift with technology, ritual and augmentation, and they carry seam-rippers that anchor them to the reality of their home, because a Drix who wanders too long without one can fall into the Weft and be lost forever.",
                        "They are deeply private. A dozen Drix in one place is a crowd, and when they do gather it is on beam worlds, planets that stay fixed whatever the state of reality and whose locations they guard jealously. On their hidden beam world they keep the Fabric of All Being, an artifact older than they are that holds the fates of all things and taught them to weftwalk. Long ago they fought beside the Fomori against the Eldrazi, and since the Fomori vanished they have hunted the remnants of that tide, using the Fabric to find and kill Eldrazi before they can rise. Their price for giving Pinnacle the stars was the right to deal with anything that comes through from outside.",
                        "Pinnacle space, to the Drix, is the cofferdam, a dry place in the middle of deep water that must always be watched for leaks. One of those sentinels, a Drix called Tumulus or Barrow, turns up at Infinite Guideline when the story needs it most. Cards like {Weftstalker Ardent} and {All-Fates Stalker} show them, and they were given a creature type all their own.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "His people watched the Eldrazi rise and the Fomori fall. Never will they watch again.",
                        "Weftblade Enhancer",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1, 6, 9 and 10, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Drix"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall, Edge of Eternities flavor text"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-the-ghost-colony", "The Ghost Colony",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-stone-speaks", "The Stone Speaks",
                    listOf(
                        "The story opens with a voice. It lies in clay until its sun explodes and shatters it, and then it starts again. It tells the reader it is something like a switch in a vast railyard, where trains split and merge and sometimes plunge into pits, but where every track in the end leads to the same place. It is lost, a long way from its railyard, and it needs someone to make choices for it. Each version of events it shows is a revision, and when the reader refuses its requests it shows the failed timeline and the dull fate waiting there, dug up, locked in stasis and handed to the Drix.",
                        "It asks for two things before the story can begin. The first is to fascinate the Illvoi scholar Mm'menon with omens, which will cost them their place in the Uthros Combine. The second concerns a stranger it calls the Metalman, who is not from around here and does not obey the ordinary rules of the railyard. Mm'menon will tell the Metalman about it, and it asks the reader to make sure the Metalman has no specialists who could retrieve it properly. It never says what it is. Later it admits it is not a god, though it was made for one to use, and that it has limits.",
                        "As the story goes on the voice lets things slip. It cannot change where things end up, only how they got there, and every change it makes has to be explained by something, by chance if nothing else. It needs a wielder who gives it a library of past moments to edit. It says that Sothera belongs to nobody, not to Pinnacle or the Kav or either faith, and that Sothera's true master loves it as it loves all the parts of itself, and will soon return to claim them. The set calls the stone {The Endstone}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The future is set. The present remains uncertain.",
                        "The Endstone",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-a-captain-and-a-cat", "A Captain and a Cat",
                    listOf(
                        "Sami is the captain of the Seriema, a tired old fusion torch that badly needs rebuilding from the keel up. They are a wildcat, an independent with a ship and no allegiance, and their crew has quit one by one, because Sami spends everything they have looking for their cat. Mirri was the very best of cats, orange and brown with a creamy white belly. Like all ordinary cats in the Edge she could weftwalk when startled, and years ago near Uthros something startled her and she vanished farther than anyone had heard of a cat going. Sami has blown every favour they ever earned looking for her.",
                        "Only Tannuk is left, a small Kav and a superb pilot, exiled from his homeworld. The flight plan Sami files with Pinnacle says the trip is a search for an escaped pet, which is true, and Pinnacle rejects it, which Tannuk expected. The real job is for the Metalman, the rudest man alive, who has sent them to land on the dwarf world Sigma and loot an empty mining colony there for something he calls an artifact. Sami suspects he chose them because they are disposable.",
                        "The Seriema comes down through Sigma's thin air out of a dead sunset, because Sothera now rises and sets as a black hole ringed with violet light. On the way down they spot a palestar, a Sunstar Free Company patrol ship, heading off to chase something else on the far side of the planet. Then they see the colony, which was supposed to be empty and dark. Every light in Sigma's Reach is on, and a sign by the runway welcomes them to a population of 13,879 plus friends and family.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "We're a few bones short of a skeleton crew, Sami, but we've got plenty of spine.",
                        "Tannuk, Steadfast Second",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-sigmas-reach", "Sigma's Reach",
                    listOf(
                        "Sigma's Reach is still sealed in its plastic wrap. Its houses were finished three years ago and wait for their families. One house cheerfully greets Sami as a dinner guest and warns that its two-year-old is shy, yet its owners never arrived. Inside, dinner is on the table and still warm. Along the main avenue Sami finds hundreds of empty slipsuits strewn like shed skins, their breathers switched on and run dry, still warm and damp inside. The ship's sensors say the air holds the heat and breath of about twenty thousand people. There are none.",
                        "Tannuk talks to the colony viy, and it explains everything. The suits blew out of a container with a bad seal. The meal was a rehearsal cooked so the food would not spoil. The warmth comes from the planet's tides. Sami does not believe a word of it. Then Sami hears a cat crying near the mining sheds and runs, past glass shapes baked into the clay that look like people twisting in agony, which the viy says are lightning strikes, on a dry world without storms.",
                        "Tannuk later notices one more strange thing. The value of moxite across all of Sothera has been sliding for ten years, as if a new mine had opened somewhere, and the only candidate is Sigma's Reach, which never opened at all. The truth comes out much later. There were people at Sigma's Reach. The Endstone, unwilling to be taken by them, edited their lives so they were never there, and Tezzeret believes they are alive elsewhere, living different lives. The glass shapes were the marks of a massacre that, in the end, never happened.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-quality-cyst", "The Quality Cyst",
                    listOf(
                        "The crying leads Sami down an access chute into a soft-walled bladder labelled quality assurance cyst zero-one, where the processing sheds dump anything they dig up and cannot identify. At the bottom is a pool of shear starch, a fluid that turns solid when struck hard, meant to protect the colony from a singularity bead or a relic of some ancient war. Something in the pool is skipping around like popcorn. It looks like an oily black egg of raw pitchblende pebbled with blisters, light bends around it in a faint pink blur, and the sounds it makes almost become a word. Chosen. Choosing one.",
                        "Sami tries to trap it in a hotcell. Then Tannuk is there, his armour burned and cracked and spiked with black glass, carrying a melted suit of armour with a face inside it, and the face is Sami's. In the struggle the stone touches Sami's gauntlet and drops into the hotcell, and Sami falls toward their own dead eyes.",
                        "Sami wakes in a bunk aboard the Seriema under thrust, back in space. Tannuk says Sami had a fit from the mould in their armour, that he found them in the cyst with a weird rock sealed in a cask, and that there were never any warships chasing them. Sami remembers a light coming down out of the sky and checking people like barcodes before they vanished, and then the memory slides away. The card {Sami's Curiosity} shows that moment of reaching for the stone, and its flavor text says Tannuk's gut warned of danger and Sami went ahead anyway. They set course for Kavaron, where the Kav keep stasis casks for exactly this sort of thing.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1 and 2, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 5, 9 and 11, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Endstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-the-dawnsire", "The Dawnsire",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-two-fallers", "Two Fallers",
                    listOf(
                        "Alpharael is a young acolyte on Susur Secundi who has lived his whole life in the monastery's shadow. He has a twin sister, Raphaella, identical except for sex, and one day the monastery chooses her for the Plummet. She is asked, and the same day she goes. Her craft, an inevitor, carries her down toward Sothera past the Dawnsire, testing whether a Monoist ship can slip through the Sunstar defences unseen, and her last transmission, pulled back from the edge of the event horizon, is a set of death poems. She confesses doubts in it and asks that her brother never be told. She agreed instantly, she writes, because a refusal would have marked him as a shirker too.",
                        "Alpharael dreams that he falls with her. In the dream he begs her to come back, says the words he never said while she was alive, and watches the tides stretch her and blood come out of her as she freezes on the burning horizon forever. He wakes certain that there is no Next Eternity, only hell. Then a messenger tells him Raphaella's flight succeeded and has proved that an inevitor can reach the Dawnsire, so an attack can go ahead.",
                        "He volunteers for it at once, saying that he wants to die, and the monastery respects his will. He spends his last night at a miners' party, talks too much, can't cry, and in the morning is given alabile armour and a sekhar bead with a whispered blessing to take it wholly so it may wholly take him. The inevitor falls toward the Dawnsire at thirty kilometres a second with no way to stop. Its passengers will survive the hit inside a cherazad, a pocket of fast time, for about ten minutes. Then the impact catches up with them, and they had better be gone into sekhar by then. Alpharael screams most of the way down, and some of the others join him.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I am worthy of the Faller's blessing. I can prove it.",
                        "Alpharael, Dreaming Acolyte",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-squire", "The Squire",
                    listOf(
                        "Aboard the shieldship Candela a squire named Haliya is wrapping her knight's leg in himsary banners and sticking her tongue out in concentration, and Syr Vondam is teasing her about it. Haliya grew up on a world where the light went out during a long diskwinter, where the people in the bunkers kept their doors shut while those outside starved and froze, and where the Solar Knights came, lit the sky and gave the sun back. Since then she has wanted nothing more than to be one of them. Vondam is calm where other knights burn, smells of argan oil rather than sweat, and has told people she is the most promising squire he has ever trained.",
                        "She eats breakfast with the other squires of her bind, Cataphrin the Cosmogrand's daughter, Isidor the complainer who is also their best fighter, Santaphor who is slow or careful, and little Quinidad the mathematician's child. They know an attack is coming and that the Monoist volunteers are coming to die. Haliya wonders aloud whether there could be a choice that is right but not good for the Sum, and nobody quite knows what to say.",
                        "Vondam takes her to the bridge of the Dawnsire, a domed golden temple to the stars where data moves as patterned light, to meet its commander, the Astelli Coronal-Captain Dark Fringes Upon Bands of Light Through a Narrow Slot, known as Slats. Slats and Vondam are old friends and trade insults happily. Then Slats tells Vondam he will die in the coming battle. Astelli prophecies are almost never wrong, and Vondam accepts it. Haliya, appalled, suggests it might be a trick, anstruth, a blasphemy against reality, a word no squire should know, just as the alarms go off.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "By the eternal and everflowing light, I swear myself to this cause.",
                        "Haliya, Ascendant Cadet",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-boarding", "The Boarding",
                    listOf(
                        "The inevitor strikes the Dawnsire and the cherazad cuts that section of the ship off from help for its ten minutes. The boarders pour out, a murderer called Purael, Nagashua, whose best friend had forgotten her, the happy Zagachoir and a grim old fighter called Simma, along with a club of volunteers who came for the joy of cracking Sunstar locks. Zagachoir is cut to pieces by mines in the first seconds and asks Alpharael to open his bead for him. Alpharael does, and his friend becomes a violet pinprick that falls away toward Sothera.",
                        "Haliya and Vondam cut down through the decks toward the fighting. The floor gives way beneath her and she lands in a bathhouse, where a Monoist comes gliding at her. It is Alpharael. Neither of them is any good at this. She shoots and her weapon explodes in her face. He shoots himself in the face with flying debris. He batters her head against the walls of her own helmet, and she cuts his armour open and hangs on to his ankle while Vondam fights Simma in the steam and kills her.",
                        "Alpharael has minutes to live, and his bead is the only door left to paradise. Instead he throws it. The tiny black hole takes his right hand with it, flies across the room and passes straight through Syr Vondam's head. The narrator stops here and asks the reader whether Alpharael deserves mercy, for choosing a few more minutes of life over eternity. It says there is only one right answer. If the reader says no, it shows the failed timeline, in which the Drix take the stone. If the reader says yes, it promises to take care of the rest, the changes to the past that will make the mercy possible.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-sentenced-to-live", "Sentenced to Live",
                    listOf(
                        "Alpharael runs and slams straight into another knight, who wants him alive for questioning. Then Captain Slats arrives, a being of pure light, and asks why he killed a friend. Alpharael says he wants to live, but as himself, not burned hollow by Sunstar interrogation. Slats tells him that in killing Vondam he was converted, that he now sees a life in this bright span of time is worth more than a long slow death, and sentences him to live. He sends Alpharael to a flight deck, where a Hopelight fighter carries him out of the cherazad just before it collapses, vitrified in hardlight to survive the acceleration.",
                        "Then it turns out Vondam is not dead. The tiny void went in beside his nose and out the back of his skull and left a scorched, perfectly round hole the size of a pinhead. Slats calls it the usual way with prophecy, you see the moment but not the context, and the bridge cheers as the Dawnsire turns its prow lasers on the Monoist monastery above Susur Secundi and scars the sky with fire that everyone in Sothera can see.",
                        "Vondam is not cheering. He knows his death was foreseen as certain, that he somehow survived, and that Slats was in exactly the right place to let the killer go. That is too many improbable things in a row. Something wants that Monoist alive, he tells Haliya, something Alpharael has touched or is about to touch. He has become anstruth, a living blasphemy, and once they are both healed they are going after the man who put a hole in his head.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "All things end. I'd rather embrace that truth than the fantasy of eternal light.",
                        "Alpharael, Stonechosen",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 3 and 4, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-kavaron-that-is", "Kavaron That Is",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-through-the-ring", "Through the Ring",
                    listOf(
                        "Sami and Tannuk dive stern first through the Mordraine Ring, dodging mountains moving at kilometres a second, with Sami cheerfully slapping the master alarm and Tannuk flying with the reflexes of a Kav trained his whole life for exactly this. Sami loves that nobody can track a ship through the Ring. Then Tannuk mentions that a Hopelight warmaker is following them, and that a Free Company palestar is chasing the Hopelight and shooting at it.",
                        "The Hopelight comes down after them with its fusion engine burning inside the atmosphere, a rolling thermonuclear fireball, and crashes on the beacon of Tannuk's old cache. Firing a fusion drive in air is wake crime, and on Kavaron the punishment is often death on the spot, but Sami cannot leave a stray. They find the fighter planted in a crater of glass. Inside is a bleeding, one-handed young man who says his name is Alpharael and that he wants to live. The narrator notes that no revision was needed to make Sami go after the wreck. That is simply who Sami is.",
                        "A Kav memorial team surrounds the crater. Their leader, a subedar, recognizes Tannuk and orders him to show his stomach. Sami pulls off their helmet and starts a con. They claim to be Sammael of a covert Monoist cutter, sent to rescue an agent who escaped the Free Company with terrible news, and Alpharael, half dead, plays along and tells the Kav that the Sunstar company plans to destroy Sothera, which is true. Sami says the weird stone in the hold is a damaged singularity bead and that they need a stasis cask to hold it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Tannuk's gut warned of danger, but Sami forged ahead without heed.",
                        "Sami's Curiosity",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-coin", "The Coin",
                    listOf(
                        "The subedar agrees to escort them to the mining town of Taro-duend, but Tannuk stays with her. Then she pulls out an old quill-shaped coin and tells Tannuk to call the flip. He will not. He chose once, he says, and chose wrong. Sami screams the call for him and the coin lands pointing at the subedar. The call goes against Tannuk, and she calls it more chance than he gave his victims.",
                        "Back on the Seriema the subedar demands proof of the bead. Sami has Alpharael move it from the hotcell into the stasis cask himself, saying no unbeliever may touch the holy instrument. Alpharael picks it up. It grows lighter in his hand and the starch around it starts falling upward, and then he is floating, free-falling along some impossible path that goes nowhere. He screams out pieces of his own story, Raphaella, the Dawnsire, Zagachoir, the wet rat of a squire who would not let go of his ankle, and then he starts praying to INEVITA to draw him down.",
                        "The subedar orders everyone killed. Every Kav in the hold groans in agony and twists inside their armour. The narrator says it is not a god and has limits, explains that the past is fluid even though the end is fixed, and allows itself a miracle. It invites the reader to give Alpharael a hand. In the next revision the Kav were never captors at all. The Hopelight's burning descent poisoned the whole memorial team with radiation, and they staggered into the Seriema begging for help. Alpharael wakes with a perfect, transparent hole through the palm of his remaining hand.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-taro-duend", "Taro-duend",
                    listOf(
                        "Sami flies twenty-four dying Kav to Taro-duend, a stilted frontier town of smokehouses, salvage yards and markets, where people trade with money and the Kav step in time on the boardwalks. The town has ambulances. Kav are a disaster culture and never give up on anyone. Here Sami learns Tannuk's story. As lead pilot controlling the Guns of Kavaron, he once saw a falling chunk of the Ring headed for the city of Summotank and its four million people. He braked it, and it fell short on the mountain villages of the Rushdown range instead, killing twenty-five thousand. To the Kav he did not save four million. He steered a disaster onto people whose turn it was not, and that makes him a murderer.",
                        "Sami goes to the town's political postilion, who turns out to be sharp and furious. He bet his fortune on moxite futures and was ruined when cheap, clean moxite from Sigma's Reach flooded the market. Sami tells him nobody ever worked at Sigma's Reach, and he cannot make sense of it. Before Sami can show him a picture of Mirri, word arrives that a Sunstar ship is overhead demanding the spies the town is hiding.",
                        "The postilion roars that he will never hand a fugitive from Taro-duend to any authority and calls the coach to start obstructing justice. The whole town rises. Kav fly kites into drones, collapse boardwalks under troops, spray foam on soldiers and stampede the checkpoints, and somehow the searchers never quite see Sami. Sami realizes the stone is lucky. Then Sami realizes what that luck might cost, that the easiest escape of all would be one where everybody in town simply vanished, the way Sigma's Reach did.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-protocol-seven", "Protocol Seven",
                    listOf(
                        "Vondam and Haliya have come in the palestar Sundog. Vondam shows her the anathalmanac. He believes the object has touched him and cannot trust his own choices, so if Alpharael reaches it, Haliya must give the order to destroy him, the object and every living thing in Taro-duend, children and animals and all. She refuses. Instead she goes down alone, wearing a triple-jointed pendulum on her helmet whose every swing chooses a passage of scripture to be prayed by billions of believers for a thousand years, so that the stone cannot swap her out without unravelling half the faith. She picked her protocol by Vondam's birth date, not her own, and she has a secret plan.",
                        "She finds the Seriema hidden in a cave and stuns Alpharael three times before he can use the rock. Then she puts a bomb in his mouth and tells him what she wants. He must use the stone to change the past so that his black hole went through her head instead of Vondam's, giving her knight his own unclouded judgement back. On the radio Vondam refuses to sell her to save himself, orders her to kill Alpharael, and then falls silent.",
                        "Sami and Tannuk come running up the ramp. Haliya cuts them both down with her bladiator, and Tannuk dies roaring in grief over his captain's body. Then Alpharael pushes Sami's jet injector through the hole in his hand, and the needle passes through Haliya's armour without piercing it and into her ankle. It is a paralytic, which Sami had labelled as seizure medicine. The stone glows pink. In the next revision Sami and Tannuk are alive, and nobody remembers dying but Haliya.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-burning", "The Burning",
                    listOf(
                        "Haliya's armour clears the drug, and she grabs Alpharael by the throat and marches him to the cockpit as the Seriema claws out of the cave with a Hopelight on its tail. Then they all see the Sundog hanging over Taro-duend like an obelisk. Every hot spot on its thermal display is a target, and its lasers service the targets one by one. Burning Kav try to smother each other and make piles of burning bodies. Those who flee indoors are let gather, and then the houses are set on fire. When the ship has recovered its troops it lights its fusion drive over the town and climbs away on a nuclear blast. Pinnacle later counts six thousand dead.",
                        "Haliya begs Vondam over the radio to kill her too, since she is closer to the object than any of the Kav. He will not. She sees that the Sum has commanded something she knows is evil, and the ground falls out from under her. Sami realizes this is what they glimpsed on Sigma, the massacre the stone had edited away.",
                        "Tannuk still has codes stolen from the subedar's armour. He calls in the Guns of Kavaron, which bounce off winged mirrors cruising in the stratosphere and burn the Hopelight out of the sky. Then Sami opens the Seriema's damaged puller sail, and the Guns, which were built to boost things rather than kill them, push the little ship out through the Ring into the dark. With every Kav navy ship now hunting the authors of the atrocity on the Seriema's cameras, nobody follows. Aboard, Sami declares their two enemies guests under mallowmass, so they cannot kill each other at the table.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The rapacious appetite that ended their world became the only means by which the Kav could lift their civilization from it.",
                        "Drill Too Deep",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 5, 6, 7 and 8, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Endstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-infinite-guideline", "Infinite Guideline",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-metalman", "The Metalman",
                    listOf(
                        "Adrift without a working drive, the Seriema gets hotter and hotter. Haliya and Alpharael argue faith in her cabin and arrive at an odd sort of truce. She decides he is an apostate, which means she can take up his case and beg his pardon from the Free Company, and therefore does not have to kill him. He gives her a bottle of ten-year-old cleaning solution and offers to pour it into her armour through the hole in his hand. She laughs.",
                        "Then a black urchin of a ship with no engines and no radiators clamps onto the Seriema out of nowhere, and the Metalman comes aboard. Tezzeret is shaped like a man but would be insulted by the comparison, a human head on a body of ciphered dark metal that crackles like a storm cloud. He is delighted by Alpharael's apostasy, pleased by Haliya's answer that she earned her metal by surviving, and quietly fascinated by Tannuk. When Haliya says she cannot let him take the object, he folds her armour around her like porridge without any visible force and sets her spinning.",
                        "He names the thing at last. It is the Endstone, the stone from the end, and he does not want it. He wants to sell it to whoever made it. It was made and then buried, he reasons, and now it wants to go home, and if something in Sothera is powerful enough to make such a thing, he means to see its face before it can make him serve it. Tezzeret has been mastered by too many dark powers to let it happen again. He drops a starry crystal on a map of Sothera to let the Endstone choose. It lands behind Uthros, on the libratory point where Infinite Guideline stands.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "What crime did you commit that makes me your best option?",
                        "Tezzeret, Mm'menon, Uthros Exile",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-checkmate", "The Checkmate",
                    listOf(
                        "On Infinite Guideline, Director Mantis, a Eumidian, is writing the report nobody wants to write. Six thousand members of a protected people have been deliberately exterminated by the Free Company. Pinnacle has no power to arrest anyone. All it can do is close the eternity column, which would trap the warring faiths in Sothera with each other. Her mechan Checkmate notices something wrong in an image of the station, and Mantis realizes a Monoist warship is hiding inside Pinnacle's own space, and that someone inside Pinnacle must have taught it how.",
                        "Her prisoner explains. Syr Vondam surrendered his ship to the Kav on the condition that he be delivered to Pinnacle, and now he sits nearly naked in a cell he has set to show a plunge into the surface of a star, a hole through his skull, waiting. He tells her that both fleets are converging on her column to keep the Endstone from escaping, hints that the Free Company has hidden its movements with help from Summists on her own crew, and that if necessary they will destroy the station and the column to keep the stone in Sothera. Mantis, a private believer in the Sum herself, resigns on the spot. Checkmate becomes Director Infinite Guideline and immediately appoints her its Checkmate.",
                        "Meanwhile Mm'menon arrives in a bubble of superfluid helium colder than space, cools the Seriema and repairs her drive. Mm'menon is rude on purpose, having learned human manners in order to break them, and explains that Sothera is a war grave, an eddy where dead things wash up from below. Sami's plan is to ask for help. They will broadcast three pleas, one to Pinnacle, one from Alpharael to the Monoists and one from Haliya to the Free Company, and steal a warp ferry while everyone waits for everyone else to move.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-three-pleas", "Three Pleas",
                    listOf(
                        "The Seriema falls toward the station at fifty kilometres a second through a battle that looks like knights jousting over a watermelon. Ships of both faiths hurl themselves past Infinite Guideline at ever greater speeds, the Monoist gravliners leave trails of ruined time behind them, and invisible mass leeches called myzoformines lie in wait for anything slow. Tannuk describes space battle as a long flight on an airliner where you do not know if you will ever land.",
                        "Alpharael sends his plea as the chosen of the singularity, twin of one who has plummeted. Haliya almost cannot do it. She sits with the detonation code for two bombs she planted on the ship back on Kavaron, and Alpharael kneels to her as a knight would be knelt to, begging her protection. She records her message. She declares herself on errantry, says she has seen crimes against the innocent committed in the faith's name, and promises to hand over the artifact only to a coronal in person. Tannuk mentions they found her bombs while the Illvoi were working and threw them out.",
                        "The fighting stops touching the Seriema. Pinnacle offers mallowmass and Sami refuses it, because they have come to steal and because they will not ask a whole station to die for them the way Taro-duend did. Docked and under quiet arrest, Sami and Tannuk go aboard to meet Checkmate Mantis and tell her the whole truth, handing over the recording of Kavaron. Mantis already knows. She says the Endstone should go to the only people who can be trusted with it, the Drix, and that one is on the station, a sentinel called Tumulus or Barrow, who has shown no interest in the stone and has spent two days building weapons.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-eld", "The Eld",
                    listOf(
                        "The lights go out. The Monoists have shot singularity beads through the station like needles and are pumping in potential matter, an artificial preparation of every possible state, which smells to Tannuk like everything at once. Sami remembers a cook on the Wurm Speaker explaining that there are cooks who use no flavour and cooks who use every flavour, and realizes they are being seasoned. Something comes in through the holes.",
                        "It is beautiful and impossible, a loop with no inside or outside, a mantle of knotted topology over a nest of arms, white porcelain plates hanging above it like a ring of standing stones. It moves in directions Sami only knows from ships entering warp. Pinnacle's war mechans shred it and it simply rotates fresh flesh in from somewhere else, and where it touches a mechan the machine is left exploded into a lattice of white chalk, every part pinned to a node of a graph. Mantis calls it an eld, which means old, dug up. Everything in Pinnacle space is too young, she says. Once in a while they find something older, and the Drix come.",
                        "Across the station more of them feed on knights, paladins, Pinnacle crews and civilians who stayed to help. They do not feed on Monoists. Not one. The story says they eat potential, the ability to become many things, and the Monoists worship only one. Pinnacle, gentle until pushed, releases its violence delegates, swarms of mechans that go for the radiators of every warship in reach. In the gardens of Third Dock, Isidor of Haliya's bind is torn apart by the tides of a gravkill paladin called the Thinner.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-squire-and-knight", "Squire and Knight",
                    listOf(
                        "Haliya and Alpharael reach the hangar where the warp ferries sleep, hundreds of them, kept here because the Kav would need so many to leave. The control viy tells them the release codes are unbreakable. Alpharael recites a long string of numbers anyway, planning to make his guess correct later with the stone, and Haliya explains at length why that is not how time works. They laugh. Then she tells him she is staying. She has to go back to the Free Company and be judged, and she has to know whether Vondam was the stone's creature when he burned Taro-duend or whether the Sum itself commanded it.",
                        "Vondam answers from behind her. He obeyed the Sum in everything, he says, except letting her go, which he did out of love. He begs her to come home. She tells him that the Sum must account for the choices people make, and that if she rewards slaughter with obedience she teaches the Sum that victory may pass through thousands of dead innocents. So she will make the Sum smaller when it does that. She will revolt.",
                        "He degrades her from his service on the spot and they fight as they always sparred, with love and no hatred. She puts herself between him and the helpless Alpharael. Alpharael crashes into her from behind and grips Vondam's neck with his burned right hand, and Haliya reaches through the hole in it, through Vondam's armour, and pulls his vertebrae apart. He begs her to kill him because he is anstruth and cannot bear it. She will not. She tells him he simply should not have murdered all those Kav, says she will never see him again, and flees. He tells her sadly that she will.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "A new dawn, burning away the darkness.",
                        "Syr Vondam, the Lucent",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-mirri", "Mirri",
                    listOf(
                        "Mantis sends her last war mechan to throw Sami and Tannuk clear and goes to lead the eld away. In a half-empty hangar Tannuk works out why their weapons fail, since most of the creature is somewhere else, and gets a dead ship's fission reactor running for something that spreads, current or fire. He needs cable. Sami tears open a stasis cask that has been sealed for more than ten years and labelled contraband, type unknown, and a pair of green-gold eyes looks back.",
                        "It is Mirri, glossy and healthy and not a day older. Years ago she weftwalked onto a ship that looked like the Seriema, landed in a stasis cask and froze, and the ship ended up at Guideline for a teardown where nobody remembered what was in the cask. Then the eld comes clattering in, and Mirri yowls and weftwalks away again. Sami, sobbing, finds cable in the next cask and drives it with their antenna rapier into the creature's bone, and the current sets it burning through dimensions it does not fit in. A second eld smashes into Tannuk from the side, and as it turns to swallow him its new flesh is already burning, and it comes apart into ash. On the ceiling stands the Drix Barrow, whose staff ends in a thirty-metre ultraviolet void rotated into the spaces where the eld's stem lives.",
                        "Barrow does not love cats, which attract predators. It mentions it has saved the beautiful insect and returned her to her post, tells them to wash off the potential matter, and is gone. Tannuk calms Sami's panic and asks his captain to choose, the Metalman's job or the search for Mirri. Sami sees that both roads lead to the same place, a ship that looks like the Seriema and a stasis cask. Back aboard they open the cask holding the Endstone. Out of the dark behind it trots a small orange and brown cat, who licks Sami's forehead and shouts when Sami tries to move.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-into-the-warp", "Into the Warp",
                    listOf(
                        "At the Seriema's hatch waits a gravkill paladin in alabile, who says the faithful will let the stone go wherever it wishes and will escort the blessed Alpharael. Tezzeret crushes it into a ball of foil from the docking tube without seeming to move and keeps the wreckage, because the machines of this place still answer him. Alpharael uses the stone, or believes he does, to make his guess at the ferry code correct. Tezzeret tells him flatly that he gave Alpharael the code himself, and cuffs him into a wall.",
                        "A Monoist inevitor has thrown a cherazad around the ship to protect it, and the ferry cannot talk to the column through the fast time. As Solar Knights leap from the dock to board, Sami takes the ship out of the bubble at full thrust straight at the heart of the station, a game of chicken against something that cannot move. The column is closed to outbound traffic. Collision is certain. The ferry and the station search every option for one that avoids disaster and find only one, which is to give Sami what they want.",
                        "The column opens, its wedge of geometry meshes with the ferry's eternity drive, and the Seriema falls redward into warp. Out there the laminae rise around them like towering cities of pure shape, the attic and cellar of the Edge, lit only by the column's beacon. Thousands are dead behind them, and Haliya will not let anyone forget it. Ahead lies the Wurmwall.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 9, 10 and 11, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Tezzeret"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Endstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-the-last-thing", "The Last Thing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-galley", "The Galley",
                    listOf(
                        "Afterwards, with wounds tended and armour stripped, they gather in the Seriema's galley over coffee Sami has been saving for a special day. Even Tezzeret comes over, cutting a hole in the hull rather than using the airlock. He toasts them. Where he comes from, he says, elder dragons, unholy machines and sphinxes all tried to master him, and he survived because of the spark inside him and the fire of possibility, the unknown made known and taken. Haliya points out that thousands are dead. Tezzeret says the dead chose their fights. Haliya reminds him of the Kav.",
                        "Tezzeret explains how he came to the Endstone. Sigma interested him as a world rich in metal and poor in miners. Then he noticed something about his own organization. It had no specialists in recovering artifacts, a mistake he would never have made, so he concluded that he must once have had them and that they had been edited out of existence when he sent them to Sigma. Whatever was there could have erased his knowledge of it too, and had not, so it wanted something from him. That is why he sent the most desperate, disposable crew he had and made it his genuine intention to take the thing wherever it wanted to go.",
                        "Sami has worked out where that is. The course runs out across the Garden of Apeiron to the Wurmwall, and Tannuk recognizes it with horror. After his exile he wanted to die and worked search and rescue in the outer system, and that is where he met Sami, on the wreck of the Wurm Speaker. Sami drags the wreck's position around its orbit on the map until it crosses their course. Tezzeret puts it plainly. They are going to the place that killed it. The Endstone sits on the table, its facets gone, now a gleaming black egg, and it rolls over to point at Tezzeret.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-android", "The Android",
                    listOf(
                        "The engine dies and the cosmos splits open in the middle of the galley. Through the gap, among galaxies and whirling pulsars, steps a beautiful creature. Its face is a green mask scaled with the oxides of eons, its ears are whorled knives, and behind the face there is only pale light. Leaves of metal wrap its slender limbs. Sami finds it beautiful like a mould that humans were pressed from, complete and finished, a being with no need to reproduce because it is the end.",
                        "The Endstone buzzes like a wasp in Alpharael's hand and falls into the creature's palm. It cups his face, calls him touched by the core and blessed, and he drops senseless. Haliya cuts off two of its fingers with an unpowered bladiator, and it crushes her throat. It touches Tezzeret's chest, says he smells of aether and tastes of the Wall, and seems curious that nothing happens. Sami, a rapier hidden under the table and a terrified cat in their lap, asks who it is. It answers that it is the last thing, and so the stone comes to it. The card {Temporal Intervention} shows this moment, and its flavor text insists it could end no other way.",
                        "It looks at Sami and speaks to them alone, about a serpent that needs a hero and a lance through its heart, and then it is gone with the stone. Haliya will live, though she needs air at once. Tezzeret holds the two severed fingers, which leak pale violet light like ichor, and smiles like a carnivore. So, he says, they do have something like a Planeswalker here. Sami picks up Mirri and holds her close. One thing is all right.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Not here. Not now. But the serpent needs a hero. Find me if you can. Put your lance through my heart. You'll come to nothing in the end.",
                        "The last thing, to Sami, Edge of Eternities, Episode 11",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-loose-threads", "Loose Threads",
                    listOf(
                        "The story ends with much unexplained, and it is careful to leave it that way. Nobody yet knows who or what the last thing is, who made the Endstone, or why it was buried under Sigma. The stone itself spoke of being made for a god's use and of Sothera's true master returning to claim its scattered parts, and the last thing simply said the stone came to it because it was the last thing. Its final words to Sami echo the dying man on the Wurm Speaker's flight deck years before, who told Sami they would come to nothing. Tezzeret, for his part, got exactly what he asked for, a glimpse of the power above him, and has not yet decided whether to flee it, bargain with it or overthrow it.",
                        "Smaller mysteries are left lying around too. Tannuk admitted to Sami that he has been lying about the Endstone, the kind of lie where he gets hurt so Sami stays safe, and said it was far too late to do anything about it. The story shows him on Sigma carrying a burned body with Sami's face, and never says more. The Garden of Apeiron is still crawling inward. Something at the core of Uthros still wants to be free. The Wurmwall still holds whatever voices the Wurm Speaker went to hear. Nobody has explained how Tezzeret was thrown out of the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities) in the first place, and Pinnacle's scholars are still puzzling over recent strange events at the Chaos Wall.",
                        "What did change is the crew. Sami has their cat. Haliya has left her order but kept her faith, on errantry as her own knight. Alpharael has given up paradise to live, Tannuk is still wary of every choice, and all of them are now wanted by both faiths and by Pinnacle. The Seriema is drifting in the warp near the Wurmwall with a busted engine and a wizard who says they will not get it fixed without him, and he has not released them.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episode 11, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 2, 5 and 9, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Endstone"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Tezzeret"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-the-crew", "The Crew",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-sami", "Sami",
                    listOf(
                        "Sami is a human wildcat captain who goes by they, pale-haired and dark-eyed, quick with their hands and quicker with their mouth. They started out as a scrapper and mechanic working the edge of the debris field around Kavaron, and before that they were kitchen help on the research ship Wurm Speaker, one of the few who came back from its voyage to the Wurmwall. They love anything you touch to make work, real switches and levers on a ship that cannot afford better. They are a liar by habit, a self-described truth engineer, and they keep their promises.",
                        "Everything Sami does is about Mirri. That devotion cost them their crew and every favour they had, and they will not apologize for it. Even if a cat is a lesser thing than a person, Sami tells Tannuk, lesser things deserve to be treated the way you would hope a god would treat you. It is also what makes them decent. They cannot leave a stray, whether it is a cat, a crashed fighter, twenty-four dying Kav or the two enemies they shelter under mallowmass.",
                        "Their cards are {Sami, Ship's Engineer}, which makes robots at the end of a turn where two creatures are tapped, and {Sami, Wildcat Captain}, a double striker that makes every spell cheaper for each artifact. Their ship is {The Seriema}, which finds a legendary creature when it arrives and makes tapped legends indestructible once crewed, much the way the ship keeps its strange passengers alive.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The hard way it is.",
                        "Sami, Wildcat Captain",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-tannuk", "Tannuk",
                    listOf(
                        "Tannuk is small for a Kav and a gentle giant to everyone else, a former command pilot of the Kav Memorial Navy and the last of Sami's crew. He is loyal past reason and very funny in a deadpan Kav way, offering to lick the mould out of Sami's armour and calling himself Tan the man. He feels the world through his belly, never forgets anything, and says aloud what he is doing in the cockpit because humans need to hear it.",
                        "He has a peculiar dread of choosing, and the story explains why. When he steered a falling piece of the Ring away from four million people and onto twenty-five thousand, he broke the deepest rule of his people, and he has refused to make a real choice ever since. After his exile he wanted to die and flew rescue missions in the outer system, which is how he found Sami in the wreck of the Wurm Speaker. Over the course of the story he lies to protect his captain about something to do with the Endstone and admits it only as a lie that is far too late to undo.",
                        "His cards are {Tannuk, Steadfast Second}, which gives other creatures haste and lets artifacts and red creatures warp in cheaply, and {Tannuk, Memorial Ensign}, which pings each opponent whenever a land enters and draws a card on the second time each turn.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "What flavor do you want, Sami? Bland, dense, or vanilla?",
                        "Tannuk, on Nutrient Block",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-haliya", "Haliya",
                    listOf(
                        "Haliya is a squire of the Sunstar Free Company who came to Sothera aboard the Dawnsire and pledged her bladiator to Syr Vondam, her mentor and idol since she was a young woman. She is serious, eager and fierce, and she bites her tongue when she concentrates. As a child she survived the diskwinter on her homeworld, where the people in the bunkers left everyone outside to freeze, and she still hates undressing in front of strangers because where she grew up being caught unarmoured could get you killed and eaten.",
                        "Her story is a crisis of faith that ends without her losing the faith. She refuses to give the order to burn a town, watches her own company do it anyway, and works out a position no Summist had taught her, that the Sum must count the people who refuse it. She is degraded and cast out by the knight she loves, cripples him rather than kill him, and places herself on errantry, a knight with no order. She also helps a cat-obsessed criminal look for a cat because it is what knights do in stories.",
                        "Her cards are {Haliya, Guided by Light}, a warp creature that gains life as things enter and draws a card when enough life has been gained, and {Haliya, Ascendant Cadet}, which spreads +1/+1 counters and draws a card when creatures carrying them deal combat damage to a player.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-alpharael", "Alpharael",
                    listOf(
                        "Alpharael is a Monoist acolyte from Susur Secundi, a pale young man with dark-lined eyes and a worried mouth. He began plagued by strange dreams after his twin sister took the Plummet, and he volunteered for a suicide mission because he wanted to follow her. At the last moment he threw away his door to paradise to live a few minutes longer, and that one choice made him the person the Endstone liked best. Fragile, the narrator says, yet close to divinity, the sibling of the eaten twin, standing right beside inevitability.",
                        "The stone left him with a hole through his right palm, a transparent ring that slowly twists, through which he can reach past any barrier. He is funny, sulky, quick to preach and quicker to doubt, and he drinks the ship's last licorice liquor. He still half believes in the Next Eternity and is terrified of going there, and what he admires in Haliya is the snarl of someone who wants to live. The last thing touched him and called him blessed before it left.",
                        "His cards are {Alpharael, Dreaming Acolyte}, which draws and discards, and {Alpharael, Stonechosen}, a void card that takes half a defending player's life when he attacks after something has left the battlefield. The extended art printing of Stonechosen carries a famous typo in its flavor text, the word the printed twice.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-tezzeret", "Tezzeret",
                    listOf(
                        "Tezzeret of Esper has served masters all his life, most of all [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), and after the Phyrexian [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) he finally had what he wanted, freedom and an indestructible body of darksteel, forged at [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn)'s command by Jin-Gitaxias. Then, by means nobody has explained, he was thrown out of the Blind Eternities and landed in the Edge.",
                        "He thrives there. His magic puts him ten steps ahead of anyone, and he spends his time fencing stolen goods, filing the numbers off the transponders of stolen ships and cornering the market in ice water, while laying the foundations of a criminal consortium. Locals call him the Metalman, though he prefers his name, and Sami thinks of him as a wizard. He is violent in body, thought and action, and he openly keeps his black urchin ship ready in case he needs to abandon everyone else. He set out wanting a way home and is no longer sure he does.",
                        "What drives him in this story is fear of being mastered again. He will not touch the Endstone. He wants to see who made it so he can decide whether to flee it, bargain with it or overthrow it. His card {Tezzeret, Cruel Captain} is a colorless three-mana planeswalker that grows whenever an artifact enters, colorless like the metal he has always loved.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I won't flee to a better world. I'll stand and face whoever would rule me. Never again will I serve.",
                        "Tezzeret, Edge of Eternities, Episode 9",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-vondam-and-slats", "Vondam and Slats",
                    listOf(
                        "Syr Vondam is a high-ranking Solar Knight who commands the host defending the Dawnsire, a storied teacher of many squires, warm and fond of a fight and fonder of his squire. He survives a black hole through the head, decides he is anstruth, and from then on refuses to trust any choice of his own. So he obeys the anathalmanac to the letter and burns Taro-duend, and then cannot bring himself to destroy the one person the protocol says he must. He gives himself up to Pinnacle to warn it, fights his squire at the end and loses, and is left helpless in the foam, begging to die.",
                        "Captain Slats, full name Coronal-Captain Dark Fringes Upon Bands of Light Through a Narrow Slot, is the Astelli who commands the Dawnsire and is coronal of the faithful aboard. He is Vondam's old friend, and the two trade insults like brothers. He foresees Vondam's death and his killer, and when he meets that killer he chooses mercy, sentencing Alpharael to live. Vondam calls it shockingly naive. Slats answers that from an Astelli view, that is why humans need the Sum.",
                        "Vondam has two cards, {Syr Vondam, Sunstar Exemplar}, which grows as your other creatures die and destroys a permanent when he falls while strong, and {Syr Vondam, the Lucent}, which hands deathtouch to the whole team.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-mmmenon-and-others", "Mm'menon and Others",
                    listOf(
                        "Mm'menon is the disgraced Illvoi researcher who became Tezzeret's right hand, a cloudsculptor and polyglot who taught him languages and annotated Pinnacle's primers for him. They are rude on purpose, perfumed, draped in sheer silks and fascinated by anomalies, and they first deduced the existence of the Endstone from data across the whole system. Their cards are {Mm'menon, the Right Hand} and {Mm'menon, Uthros Exile}.",
                        "The set has more legends than the story has room for. {Xu-Ifit, Osteoharmonist} is a Monoist surgeon whose wife took the Plummet ten years ago and was frozen in place by a gravitational anomaly, and who experiments on acolytes in the hope of saving her. {Dyadrine, Synthesis Amalgam} is a mechan abandoned on a barren moon who repaired themself with the local plants until plant and machine became one. {Ragost, Deft Gastronaut} is a lobsterfolk chef who claims to have done everything, including communing with the star god at the core of Uthros, and now cooks for a crew of mechans who want to learn to taste.",
                        "Some figures appear only in the story. Director Mantis of Infinite Guideline is a Eumidian who resigned rather than run a station her own faith had compromised, and survived the eld thanks to a Drix. Raphaella, Alpharael's twin, is frozen on Sothera's horizon. Simma, Zagachoir and Purael died on the Dawnsire, and the bind of squires Haliya ate breakfast with is scattered across a war.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "They will call you a monster, but I call you worthy.",
                        "Xu-Ifit, Osteoharmonist",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Edge of Eternities, by Miguel Lopez and others, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Edge of Eternities, Episodes 1 to 11, by Seth Dickinson, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Man of Parts, by Reinhardy Suarez, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Tezzeret"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-spacecraft-and-station", "Spacecraft and Station",
                    listOf(
                        "Edge of Eternities added a new artifact type, Spacecraft, for the warships, freighters and cruisers of Sothera, and every Spacecraft has the new keyword station. Station is an activated ability you use only at sorcery speed. You tap another untapped creature you control, even one that just arrived, and put charge counters on the Spacecraft equal to that creature's power. The crew go to their stations and the ship powers up.",
                        "The text box of a Spacecraft is striped, and each stripe has a threshold printed in a circle. Once the ship has at least that many charge counters it gains the abilities in that stripe, and when the highest stripe shows a power and toughness, the ship becomes an artifact creature too. {Wurmwall Sweeper} becomes a 2/2 flier at four counters. {Lumen-Class Frigate} pumps your team at two and becomes a flying lifelinker at twelve. A few ships never become creatures at all, like {The Eternity Elevator}, which simply becomes a colossal mana engine at twenty.",
                        "Unlike Vehicles, a Spacecraft keeps its counters and stays a creature permanently once powered. The set also changed the Commander rules, so that from Edge of Eternities on, any legendary Vehicle or legendary Spacecraft with a printed power and toughness can lead a deck. That is how ships like {Inspirit, Flagship Vessel} and {Hearthhull, the Worldseed} became commanders in the set's own preconstructed decks.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-planets", "Planets",
                    listOf(
                        "Planet is a new land type. It carries no rules of its own, but every Planet in the set has station, and a cycle of five mythic rare Planets stands for the five worlds of Sothera. Each enters tapped, taps for one color, and gains a powerful ability once it holds twelve or more charge counters, so you send your creatures to work the land instead of attacking.",
                        "Each payoff fits its world. {Adagia, Windswept Bastion}, the Sunstar colony, makes legendary token copies of your artifacts and enchantments. {Uthros, Titanic Godcore} taps for blue mana for each artifact you control. {Susur Secundi, Void Altar} sacrifices a creature to draw cards equal to its power. {Kavaron, Memorial World} sacrifices a land to make a robot and send your army in hasty. {Evendo, Waking Haven} taps for green mana for every creature you control.",
                        "A card called {Drill Too Deep} can put five charge counters on a Spacecraft or Planet in one go, and its flavor text is about the mining that destroyed Kavaron. The set also reprinted the five allied shock lands, each with flavor text from the travel log of an explorer named Maisie, and gave every color access to {Command Bridge}, a land that taps for any color.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-warp", "Warp",
                    listOf(
                        "Warp is an alternative cost found mostly on creatures, though it can appear on any permanent. You may cast a warp card from your hand for its warp cost instead of its mana cost, usually much cheaper. If you do, the permanent is exiled at the beginning of the next end step, and on a later turn you may cast it from exile for its full cost. It is a ship flashing in from faster than light, doing its job and leaving, then coming back for good.",
                        "Because the warped permanent is around only briefly the first time, warp cards tend to do something when they arrive. {Starfield Shepherd} fetches a Plains or a cheap creature for two mana and comes back later as a 3/3 flier. {Exalted Sunborn} can warp in for two to double the tokens you make that turn. {Anticausal Vestige}, an Eldrazi, warps in for four, leaves, and draws you a card and drops a permanent from your hand as it goes. Only the hand can be warped from, unless a card like {Timeline Culler} says otherwise, and mana value always comes from the printed mana cost.",
                        "Some cards care whether a spell was warped this turn, whoever cast it and whether or not it resolved. {Tannuk, Steadfast Second} gives warp to every artifact and red creature in your hand, which turns the whole deck into a ship that flickers in and out.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-void-and-landers", "Void and Landers",
                    listOf(
                        "Void is an ability word for the space left behind. A void ability checks whether a nonland permanent has left the battlefield this turn or a spell was warped this turn, and either one is enough, no matter who controlled it. On instants and sorceries void usually makes the effect bigger, as when {Tragic Trajectory} turns from minus two into minus ten. On permanents it is usually an end step trigger, as on {Insatiable Skittermaw}, which grows each turn something disappeared, or {Elegy Acolyte}, which makes a robot. The Monoists live in the black and red void decks, and Alpharael is one of their payoffs.",
                        "Lander is a new predefined token, joining Treasure and Food. A Lander is a colorless artifact with the ability to pay two, tap and sacrifice it to search your library for a basic land and put it onto the battlefield tapped. Ships making planetfall, scouts and Eumidian seed pods all make them. They fix mana, feed landfall, and count as artifacts, which the set uses heavily.",
                        "Edge of Eternities also introduced the Drix as a creature type, and its rules leaned on familiar returning ideas, landfall for the Eumidians, +1/+1 counters for Sunstar and Evendo, and caring about the second spell each turn for the Illvoi and the Sunstar knights.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-draft-archetypes", "Draft Archetypes",
                    listOf(
                        "The set was built for Play Boosters, with each two-color pair given a direction and a pair of uncommon signposts. White and blue cares about casting a second spell each turn, signposted by {Station Monitor}. Blue and black is artifact control, led by {Alpharael, Dreaming Acolyte}. Black and red is the void deck, with {Interceptor Mechan}. Red and green is Landers and landfall, led by {Tannuk, Memorial Ensign}. Green and white is +1/+1 counters, with {Haliya, Ascendant Cadet}.",
                        "The enemy pairs go on. White and black goes wide, signposted by {Syr Vondam, the Lucent}. Blue and red is artifact aggression, with {Mm'menon, Uthros Exile}. Black and green works the graveyard, with {Seedship Broodtender}. Red and white is the space station deck, tapping creatures to power ships, led by {Sami, Ship's Engineer}. Green and blue ramps, with {Biomechan Engineer}.",
                        "The story's crew are spread across those signposts. Sami runs the red and white station deck and Tannuk the red and green Landers. Haliya leads the green and white counters, Vondam the white and black swarm, Alpharael the blue and black artifacts, and Mm'menon the blue and red artifact aggression. The set's story spotlight cards, which illustrate scenes from the fiction, include {Command Bridge}, {Gravkill}, {Honor}, {Luxknight Breacher}, {Pain for All}, {Radiant Strike} and {The Seriema}.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Edge of Eternities Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Spacecraft"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-stone-and-the-star", "The Stone and the Star",
                    listOf(
                        "Two cards stand at the heart of the set. {Sothera, the Supervoid} was the set's headliner, a four-mana legendary enchantment that makes each opponent exile a creature whenever one of yours dies, and once a player has no creatures left it collapses and spits out one of the exiled creatures for you, bigger than before. It is a black hole on the table, eating everything around it.",
                        "{The Endstone} is a seven-mana legendary artifact that draws a card every time you play a land or cast a spell, a library of futures opening up, and then at the beginning of each of your end steps sets your life total to half your starting life. In Commander that means twenty no matter how high or low you were, which can save a dying player or cost a healthy one dearly. Its flavor text sums up the story's physics. The future is set. The present remains uncertain.",
                        "Around them sit the ships and places of the story. {The Seriema} fetches a legend, {Dawnsire, Sunstar Dreadnought} deals one hundred damage when you attack, and {Infinite Guideline Station}, a five-color Spacecraft, makes robots and draws cards for every multicolored permanent you control. {Temporal Intervention} and {Sami's Curiosity} both show the Endstone in their art.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-the-legends", "The Legends",
                    listOf(
                        "{Tezzeret, Cruel Captain} is the set's planeswalker, colorless and three mana, which makes him playable in any Commander deck. He gains loyalty every time an artifact enters, untaps things, tutors cheap artifacts, and his ultimate turns artifacts into growing robots. The crew mostly come in pairs, a cheaper uncommon and a rare or mythic, so {Sami, Wildcat Captain}, {Tannuk, Steadfast Second}, {Haliya, Guided by Light}, {Alpharael, Stonechosen} and {Mm'menon, the Right Hand} each have an uncommon partner from the draft signposts.",
                        "Mm'menon, the Right Hand lets you look at the top of your library at any time and cast artifacts from it, and turns your artifacts into blue mana for doing so. Haliya, Guided by Light became one of the most played cards of the set in Commander, as a lifegain engine that refills your hand. {Ragost, Deft Gastronaut} turns every artifact into Food and every Food into three damage to each opponent, and {Xu-Ifit, Osteoharmonist} reanimates a creature each turn as a skeleton with no abilities, which sounds weak until you put it in a deck built to care.",
                        "Other legends from the set include {Dyadrine, Synthesis Amalgam}, a green and white X spell of counters and robots, and the two Syr Vondams. The Commander decks add four more legends, covered on their own tab.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-commander-staples", "Commander Staples",
                    listOf(
                        "For Commander players the set's biggest draws were its reprints and a handful of engines. The five shock lands, {Breeding Pool}, {Godless Shrine}, {Sacred Foundry}, {Stomping Ground} and {Watery Grave}, came back with new art and flavor text from an explorer's log of the moons of the Edge. Beyond them, the set's most played cards in Commander include {Icetill Explorer}, which lets you play an extra land and play lands from your graveyard, and {Terrasymbiosis}, which draws cards when you put +1/+1 counters on creatures.",
                        "{Exalted Sunborn} doubles tokens and can warp in for two. {Ouroboroid} puts +1/+1 counters equal to its own power on each creature you control every combat, which spirals quickly. {Starfield Vocalist} doubles the triggers of permanents entering. {Quantum Riddler} draws an extra card whenever your hand is nearly empty. {Loading Zone} doubles counters on creatures, Spacecraft and Planets. {Archenemy's Charm} is a flexible triple black spell, and {Weftwalking} lets every player cast their first spell each turn for free.",
                        "On the destructive side, {Singularity Rupture} destroys every creature and mills half of any number of libraries, {Zero Point Ballad} wipes small creatures and can steal one back, and {Pinnacle Starcage} exiles every cheap artifact and creature until it leaves. {Famished Worldsire} eats your lands and gives you more back, as a leviathan should.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-old-terrors", "Old Terrors",
                    listOf(
                        "The Edge is a young universe surrounded by old things, and the cards bring some of them back. The Drix fought the Eldrazi at the side of the Fomori long ago, and {Anticausal Vestige} is a six-mana colorless Eldrazi with warp. The flavor text of {Bygone Colossus}, a nine-mana robot giant that warps in for three, says the Edge is littered with the remains of civilizations the Eldrazi annihilated.",
                        "Slivers are here too. {Thrumming Hivepool} is an artifact that grants Slivers double strike and haste and makes two Sliver tokens every upkeep, and its flavor text is a decrypted log by someone who fears the slivers will escape their rock. In the Edge, slivers are the stuff of horror films, and nobody believes they are real. The Alchemy cards on Arena added a Sliver Weftwinder that tunnels through the Weft and lives in the Chaos Wall.",
                        "And the lhurgoyf got a space suit. {Cosmogoyf} has power equal to the number of cards you own in exile, and its flavor text is the final transmission of a ship's computer, recommending that the crew run.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        ">Alert: COSMOGOYF / >Recommended Action: RUN",
                        "PSS Erix shipboard computer, final transmission, Cosmogoyf",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "EDHREC rankings via Scryfall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eoe-commander-and-extras", "Commander and Extras",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-counter-intelligence", "Counter Intelligence",
                    listOf(
                        "Edge of Eternities came with two Commander decks in its regular product line. Counter Intelligence is blue, red and white and is led by {Kilo, Apogee Mind}, a hasty three-mana robot that proliferates every time it becomes tapped, which in a deck full of stations, counters and tappers happens a lot. Its other legend is the ship itself, {Inspirit, Flagship Vessel}, which puts a +1/+1 counter or two charge counters on another artifact each combat and, once fully powered, makes your other artifacts hexproof and indestructible.",
                        "The story behind the deck is one of the strangest in the set. The Inspirit was once the pride of a distant world, an eternity ship launched and lost to the hazards of Weft travel, and it now drifts in the Garden of Apeiron, the region where ships go in and never come out. Its navigational mind became Kilo, which learned to set itself apart from the vessel, named itself, and moved the main sliver of its consciousness into a single mechan body.",
                        "Kilo cherishes consciousness and wants to share it. Mechan pilgrims find their way aboard the Inspirit, and each one takes a splinter of Kilo's power into itself, a seed that encourages willing minds to grow. To those pilgrims the ship is a temple and the body of a god. New cards in its colors like {Patrolling Peacemaker} and {Uthros Research Craft} lean on proliferating and artifacts.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Both sculptor and sculpted.",
                        "Kilo, Apogee Mind",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-world-shaper", "World Shaper",
                    listOf(
                        "World Shaper is black, red and green and is led by {Szarel, Genesis Shepherd}, a flying Eumidian who lets you play lands from your graveyard and spreads +1/+1 counters whenever you sacrifice something on your turn. Its second legend, {Hearthhull, the Worldseed}, is a Spacecraft that sacrifices lands to draw cards and makes each opponent lose life whenever you do. The deck is about destroying your own land and building it back, terraforming as a strategy.",
                        "In the fiction Szarel is the lead officer of the current stage of terrasymbiosis on Evendo, managing the remaking of a world while defending it from Kav colonists, knowing full well he will not live to see the work finished. The Hearthhull is a colossal transport that is technically an ancient living Eumidian, grown over centuries large enough to carry a whole brood between stars. Unlike other seedships it is conscious and feels a fierce attachment to the brood it planted on Evendo, and as long as it watches over the planet the brood has a fighting chance against the Kav.",
                        "The new Commander cards in its colors fit the theme, from {Planetary Annihilation}, which leaves every player six lands and burns every creature, to {Eumidian Hatchery}, a land that leaves flying insects behind when it dies, and {Scouring Swarm}, which copies itself when your graveyard fills with lands.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The better you understand how a planet is made, the better you understand how one is unmade.",
                        "Ulix, Eumidian terrademolitionist, Planetary Annihilation",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "eoe-stellar-sights", "Stellar Sights",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's bonus sheet was Edge of Eternities Stellar Sights, forty-five famous lands from Magic's history reprinted with new art of the wonders of space. It ranges from Commander staples like {Ancient Tomb}, {Mana Confluence}, {Reflecting Pool} and {Gemstone Caverns} to {Strip Mine}, {Mutavault}, {Inkmoth Nexus} and {Eldrazi Temple}, along with all ten of the two-color creature lands from the Zendikar blocks, such as {Celestial Colonnade} and {Raging Ravine}.",
                        "The sheet was graded like a set of its own. Lands such as {Ancient Tomb}, {Strip Mine}, {Lotus Field} and {Plaza of Heroes} were mythic on it, while utility lands like {Blast Zone}, {Thespian's Stage} and {Scavenger Grounds} were rare. For Commander players the sheet was a quiet treasure, since several of its lands rank among the most played in the format.",
                        "There were also smaller oddities. The printed {Diplomatic Relations} lost part of its rules text, so it read target creature rather than target creature you control, and Wizards put out a statement on how it should be played. The extended art {Genemorph Imago} got the crown flourish of a legendary card despite not being legendary. And the digital Alchemy cards on Arena introduced more faces of Sothera, among them the rogue Kav miner Thendar, the marooned Pinnacle surveyor Val, the Drix assassin Axavar who cut his own fate free, and Mm'menon's Combine rival Vv'viza.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities Commander"),
                StorySource("Set", "Edge of Eternities Stellar Sights"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Edge of Eternities, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Edge of Eternities"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
